Subject: [PATCH 0/3] mm: batch file VMA insertion during fork

This series batches file VMA insertion during fork. The first patch shares
batch storage conventions with unlink_file_vma and inserts up to eight
VMAs for one address_space under a single i_mmap write lock. File VMAs
needing page-table copying are linked before copy_page_range, and all
remaining entries are linked before successful completion or teardown.

The second patch adds file VMA lifetime tests. The third keeps four active
mapping batches, allowing interleaved files to accumulate independently,
and extends those tests to four and five files, including private COW.
It retains synchronous page-table copying and the existing flush points.

In an x86_64 QEMU diagnostic, 64 VMAs interleaved over two or four files
used eight insertion batches. The preceding single-batch implementation
would use 64 for these sequences. A five-file, 65-VMA sequence used 32
batches, reflecting the bounded four-slot cache. The buffers grow from
136 to 544 bytes on x86_64, with no new allocation. UnixBench spawn kept
its prior 4+1 batch shape per file; its layout does not gain additional
coalescing from the extra slots.

Validation used KASAN and lockdep: 16 file VMA selftests passed, guard
checks passed five with four skips, SysV SHM lifetime checks passed, and
128 overcommit-induced fork failures passed cleanup and write-seal checks.
Traces verified homogeneous batches, complete insertion, and insertion
before required file page-table copies, with no lost events or probe hits.
The kernel builds and all three patches pass strict checkpatch.

These measurements establish batching behavior, not hardware throughput
improvements. UFFD, hugetlb, device-specific callbacks and other
architectures have not been exercised at runtime for this revision.

The changes and validation were prepared with LLM assistance. The batch
tracing and failure-injection diagnostics are local project artifacts;
the upstream-facing tests check inherited contents, truncate and sealing.

Dong Chenchen (3):
  mm: batch file VMA insertions in dup_mmap
  selftests/mm: cover file VMA lifetime across fork
  mm: keep multiple file VMA batches in dup_mmap

